  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 300 AM EDT Monday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Hot conditions prevail during the early to \nmiddle portion of the week. Isolated storms possible, but mostly \ndry. \n\nA late-summer heat wave is imminent as a strong upper level ridge \nbuilding over the Mississippi River valley translates toward the east \ncoast. Temperatures rise through the week, starting with highs in \nthe low 90s today and peaking near 100 on Wed/Thurs. Still hesitant \nto put widespread 100F+ in the forecast as the NBM suggests, but \nconfidence is growing in this solution given the statistical models \nstarting to pick up on it as well. If realized, it would definitely \nbreak a few max temp records for early September. Unfortunately, it \nwill also feel humid and stagnant on top of the already hot \nconditions with strong sfc high pressure leading to little to no \nwind and dew points in the upper 60s and low 70s. Heat indices in \nthe upper 90s and low 100s are expected today and Tues, then 100-105 \non Wed/Thurs. Heat Advisories are likely for at least part of the \narea these days; highest confidence in this is E of I-95. \nOvernight lows will not provide much relief, especially Wed \nnight when lows will be in the upper 70s.\n\nIt will be mostly dry through mid-week, but a few isolated \nshowers/storms will be possible today and late tomorrow as \nshortwaves travel down the ridge. Given the NW flow aloft and \nno short supply of instability, stronger storms are possible,\nbut confidence is pretty low at the moment. SPC has N/NE \nportions of the area in a Marginal Risk for Tuesday. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Shower/storm chances increase late week as a \ncold front approaches. Cooler, but seasonable, temps forecast behind \nthe front. \n\nThe strong ridge aloft starts to break down/flatten late in the week \nand a sfc cold front approaches from the north, passing through the \narea sometime Fri-Fri night as of latest modeling. Afternoon and \nevening thunderstorms are forecast Thurs and Fri ahead of and along \nthe front. Based on the 00z global models, Fri looks like the \nhighest coverage day. NW flow will still be set up aloft, so \nthe potential for severe storms certainly exists. As with most \nset ups around here, it will come down to timing and whether \nthere is enough instability around when convection gets going. \nThe local FA is not currently outlooked in SPC's extended \nforecast product. We should see a little bit of relief from the \nheat over the weekend, but still seasonably warm with highs in \nthe low 80s.",
